Q: How to pause C# code for ten minutes at the start of each hour A: <p>You wish to continue with your current pattern of using <code>Thread.Sleep(n)</code>, can use a simple loop.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>    using System.Threading;

    //...
    while (DateTime.Now.Minute &lt;= 10)
    {
        Thread.Sleep(1000);
    }
</code></pre>
<p>In general though it would be better to schedule the task or at least to use <code>async</code> instead of blocking the thread.</p>

Q: keySystem in libgdx like Granny A: <p>It's difficult to answer you, because the question is very big and vague.</p>
<p>This an example of the architecture of a similar game written for another ECS framework, but they all have the same essence. Removed unnecessary information and added variables from the question</p>
<p>Everything is based on four <strong>basic concepts</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Game <strong>object</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Component</strong> - stores all information about the object.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>System</strong> - processes user logic for objects with one or more components.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>World</strong> in which objects and systems of objects live.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Architecture</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Component</p>
</li>
<li><p>Data Layer</p>
</li>
<li><p>Spawn Manager</p>
</li>
<li><p>Entity Factory</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>How it works.</strong> <em>Approximately. <strong>Very Approximately.</strong></em></p>
<p>1. In Blender, you create empty <strong>nodes</strong> (<em>weapon_key, shield_key, small_key</em>) inside <strong>boxes</strong>. When loading the scene, each such node becomes a separate entity in ECS.</p>
<p>2. <strong>Component</strong> for object spawn points - marks boxes as possible object spawn points for specific types of keys.</p>
<p>Component with <code>keyType = &quot;weapon_key&quot;</code> (example) is attached to each <code>weapon_key</code> node at the moment of entity creation when loading the scene.</p>
<pre><code>public class PointComponent implements Component {

    public String keyType;  

    public Vector3 localOffset = new Vector3();

}
</code></pre>
<p>3. <strong>Data Layer</strong>. (singleton)</p>
<p>A list of the object's bindings points. It is created at the start of the level and collects all such points into an object. Is filled in when the level is initialized. Just go through all the entities and select the ones that have a <code>PointComponent</code>.</p>
<pre><code>public class PointCollection {

    private final Map&lt;String, List&lt;Entity&gt;&gt; pointsByType = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();

    public void register(Entity entity, String keyType) {
        pointsByType.computeIfAbsent(keyType, k -&gt; new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()).add(entity);
    }

    public List&lt;Entity&gt; getPointsFor(String keyType) {
        return pointsByType.getOrDefault(keyType, Collections.emptyList()); // (key type, list points)
    }

}
</code></pre>
<p>4. <strong>Key Manager</strong> (singleton)</p>
<p>His task is (once at the start of the level) to <em>find points -&gt; select a point -&gt; create a key for it</em>.</p>
<pre><code>public class KeyManager {

    private final PointCollection collection;
    private final EntityFactory entityFactory;
    private final Random random;

    public KeyManager(PointCollection collection,
                      EntityFactory entityFactory,
                      Random random) {

        this.collection = collection;
        this.entityFactory = entityFactory;
        this.random = random;

    }

    /**
     * Creates exactly one key of the specified type at a random point.
     */
    public Entity spawnKeyOnce(String keyType, World world) {

        List&lt;Entity&gt; points = collection.getPointsFor(keyType);

        if (points.isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(&quot;No points found&quot;);
        }

        int index = random.nextInt(points.size());
        Entity spawnPointEntity = points.get(index);

        // get the position of the point of appearance in the world
        TransformComponent transform = spawnPointEntity.getComponent(TransformComponent.class);
        Vector3 worldPos = new Vector3(transform.position);

        // Creating a key through the factory
        Entity keyEntity = entityFactory.createKey(keyType, worldPos);
        world.addEntity(keyEntity);

        return keyEntity;

    }

}
</code></pre>
<p>5. <strong>Factory</strong> (EntityFactory)</p>
<p>The factory is only responsible for creating key entity by position. She doesn't have to decide where and when to create. <strong>The system does not know of the factory, and the factory does not know about randomization.</strong></p>
<pre><code>public class EntityFactory {

    public Entity createKey(String keyType, Vector3 position) {

        Entity entity = new Entity();
        entity.add(new TransformComponent(position));

    // Component key
        entity.add(new KeyComponent(keyType)); 

        // here you can add a model, a render, etc.

        return entity;

    }

}
</code></pre>
<p>6.When loading a level, we collect points</p>
<pre><code>public class LevelInit{

    private final PointCollection collection;
    private final World world;
    private final EntityFactory entityFactory;
    private final Random random;

    public LevelInit(World world,
                            PointCollection collection,
                            EntityFactory entityFactory,
                            Random random) {

        this.world = world;
        this.collection = collection;
        this.entityFactory = entityFactory;
        this.random = random;

    }

// Full initialization of the level

    public void initLevel(LevelConfig config) {

        //collecting points

        ImmutableArray&lt;Entity&gt; entities = world.getEntitiesFor(ComponentType.get(PointComponent.class));

        for (Entity e : entities) {

            PointComponent spc = e.getComponent(PointComponent.class);

            // Registering a point under its key type
            collection.register(e, spc.keyType);

        }

        // create keys

        KeyManager manager = new KeyManager(collection, entityFactory, random);

        // spawn one key of each type specified in the config.

        for (String keyType : config.keyTypesToSpawn) {

            manager.spawnKeyOnce(keyType, world);

        }

    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>7. Array of key types</p>
<pre><code>public class Config {

    public final String[] keyTypesToSpawn;

    public Config(String... keyTypes) {
        this.keyTypesToSpawn = keyTypes;
    }

}
</code></pre>
<p>8. In class <strong>Main</strong> (example, in create() or in the level transition handler)</p>
<pre><code>World world = engine.getWorld();
SpawnCollection collection = new SpawnCollection();
EntityFactory factory = new EntityFactory();
Random random = new Random();

LevelInit init = new LevelInit(world, collection, factory, random);

Config config = new Config(&quot;weapon_key&quot;, &quot;shield_key&quot;, &quot;small_key&quot;);

init.initLevel(config);
</code></pre>
<p>In general, everything.</p>
<p>If this architecture is suitable for you, then specify what is unclear and I will describe these details in more detail.</p>
<p>If you type <code>LibGDX</code> or <code>Artemis</code> in the GitHub search, you can find many interesting solutions to your question.</p>
